    "@id": "https://api.nuget.org/v3/catalog0/data/2026.09.27.17.55.19/invarix.gate.agentframework.1.0.0-rc.1.json#deprecation",
    "alternatePackage": {
      "@id": "https://api.nuget.org/v3/catalog0/data/2026.09.27.17.55.19/invarix.gate.agentframework.1.0.0-rc.1.json#deprecation/alternatePackage",
      "id": "Invarix.Gate.AgentFramework",
      "range": "[1.0.0-rc.3, )"
    },
    "message": "Superseded by 1.0.0-rc.3 of the Invarix.Gate packages. rc.3 fixes approval requests that could carry credentials unmasked (advisory INVARIX-2026-001, https://invarix.dk/security), an Agent Framework defect that, in enforce mode and with no GateRunScope, terminated every run once the agent's total tool calls passed the per-run ceiling, and the evidence recorded for approvals nobody answered. The rc.2 README's example policy ran 3 of the 11 detectors; re-check any policy copied from it.",
    "reasons": [
      "Other",
      "CriticalBugs"
    ]
  },
  "description": "Microsoft Agent Framework adapter for Invarix.Gate, the deterministic action firewall for AI agents on .NET. One call, agent.AsBuilder().UseInvarixGate(gate).Build(), routes the agent's tool calls through Gate's permission envelope before they execute. Token accounting needs the chat boundary tapped, and Agent Framework's builder cannot install that: wrap the IChatClient with UseInvarixGate from Invarix.Gate.Extensions.AI before constructing the agent. At attach time this package enumerates the agent's registered tools and warns about any that cannot be intercepted, so a wiring mistake surfaces at startup instead of during an incident. Observe mode is the default and blocks nothing.",
  "iconFile": "nuget-icon-128.png",
  "id": "Invarix.Gate.AgentFramework",
